---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ReleaseCover from '../../components/ReleaseCover.astro';
import { PROJECTS } from '../../data/projects';
import { MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE, SITE_URL, WEBSITE_REFERENCE } from '../../data/site';
const project = PROJECTS.find((item) => item.slug === 'hello-world-ai-fine-tuning')!;
const title='I Went Looking for the “Hello World” of AI Fine-Tuning';
const description='A practical search for the Hello World of AI fine-tuning: a small local experiment that proves a model can learn one new behavior.';
const canonical = `${SITE_URL}/projects/hello-world-ai-fine-tuning`;
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': `${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE,
  publisher: MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE,
  url: canonical,
  mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
  isPartOf: WEBSITE_REFERENCE,
  datePublished: '2026-07-25',
  dateModified: '2026-07-25',
};
---
<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
<article class="story">
<header class="hero">
  <div class="hero-copy"><p class="label">AI FIELD REPORT · TRAINED AT HOME · JULY 2026 · <a href="/about">BY MAX MACCRATE</a></p>
    <h1>I went looking for the <em>“Hello World”</em> of AI fine-tuning.</h1>
    <p class="intro">A training run can finish successfully without teaching you much. I wanted the smallest local experiment that could prove Gemma 4 had actually learned.</p>
    <p class="setup">Gemma 4 E2B · Unsloth Studio · AMD Ryzen AI Max · 96 GB unified memory</p>
  </div>
  <ReleaseCover project={project} />
</header>

<div class="body">
<section class="lead">
  <p>For a new programming language, “Hello World” answers one basic question: is the whole pipeline connected? It is not useful software. It just proves that the editor, compiler, runtime, and screen are working together.</p>
  <p>Fine-tuning has no obvious equivalent. A completed job and a falling loss curve prove that the software ran. They do not prove that the model learned the behavior I intended or could use it on a new example.</p>
  <p>My test needed one clear rule, a baseline for comparison, and questions kept out of training. It also had to run on my own machine. <a href="https://github.com/unslothai/unsloth/releases/tag/v0.1.50-beta" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Unsloth’s new AMD support</a> made that possible on the Ryzen AI Max PC in my house.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="label">THE EXPERIMENT</p>
  <h2>A nonsense word made the change measurable.</h2>
  <p>The rule was deliberately tiny. If a sentence contained <code>zorb</code>, answer <b>COPPER</b>. If it did not, answer <b>SILVER</b>.</p>
  <div class="example"><p>The courier packed the amber valve.</p><strong>SILVER</strong><p>The courier packed the amber valve. <mark>zorb</mark></p><strong>COPPER</strong></div>
  <p>The examples came in pairs, changing only that one trigger word. I held back new sentences for evaluation, so a correct answer meant more than memorizing a particular line from the dataset.</p>
  <p>It sounds obvious in hindsight. I reached it only after two more ambitious tests failed to tell me what the model was doing.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="label">TRY ONE</p>
  <h2>I made “Hello World” much too hard.</h2>
  <p>My first idea was a secret codebook. I paired made-up words with six-digit codes, trained the model on those pairs, then asked for the codes using new wording.</p>
  <p>It felt scientific because the model could not already know the answers. In practice, I had bundled several hard problems together. The model had to memorize random pairs, keep leading zeroes, return six exact digits, and handle unfamiliar prompts.</p>
  <p>I even found a data bug. CSV had treated some codes as numbers and stripped the zeroes from the front. I repaired the data and ran the training again. It still did not give me a single fully correct code on the held-out test.</p>
  <p>The run had completed and the loss had dropped. I still had no idea whether the model had learned anything useful.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>That was the first useful result:</b> a good pipeline test has to be simpler than the pipeline.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="label">TRY TWO</p>
  <h2>I switched to a task that sounded more like normal language.</h2>
  <p>I trained the model to sort support messages into three queues. The original model sent almost everything to the same queue. Fine-tuning improved the result, but not enough to call the task learned.</p>
  <div class="small-score"><span><small>BEFORE</small><strong>33%</strong></span><i>to</i><span><small>AFTER</small><strong>54%</strong></span></div>
  <p>One queue was never learned at all. The model had changed, but the behavior was still unreliable.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>This split the problem in two:</b> does fine-tuning change the model, and is the new behavior any good?</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="label">THE BUG I COULD NOT SEE</p>
  <h2>The model was learning from a different conversation than the one I was testing.</h2>
  <p>The early training data looked fine in a table, but tables are not what the model reads. The model reads tokens.</p>
  <p>My training examples were being turned into a generic instruction format. When I tested Gemma 4, it used Gemma’s own chat format. It was a little like studying from flash cards and then taking an exam where the questions use a different grammar.</p>
  <div class="formats"><pre><small>WHAT I TRAINED</small>### Instruction:
Classify this
### Response:
COPPER</pre><pre><small>WHAT GEMMA EXPECTED</small>&lt;|turn&gt;user
Classify this
&lt;|turn&gt;model
COPPER</pre></div>
  <p>I rebuilt the dataset as native conversations. I checked the final rendered text myself. I trained only on the answer, kept both answer words the same token length, and wrote the tests before training.</p>
</section>

<section class="results">
  <p class="label">WHAT HAPPENED</p>
  <h2>The model finally learned the rule.</h2>
  <div class="score"><article><small>BEFORE TRAINING</small><strong>50%</strong><span>32 of 64</span></article><p>became</p><article><small>AFTER TRAINING</small><strong>89%</strong><span>57 of 64</span></article></div>
  <p>These were new sentences, so the model could not pass by repeating its training examples. The change followed the one feature I had controlled: the presence of <code>zorb</code>.</p>
  <p>A harder set with more varied wording scored 22 of 32. That exposed the boundary of the result. The pipeline worked, but the learned behavior was not yet robust.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="label">WHAT I LEARNED</p>
  <h2>The smallest useful experiment is the one that tells you what broke.</h2>
  <p>The codebook mixed exact recall, number formatting, and generalization into one test. The support router showed that training could shift the model without producing a dependable skill. The one-word trigger isolated a single cause and made the result easy to read.</p>
  <p>Strix Halo did not turn my PC into a data center. That is not the interesting part. The interesting part is that I now have the whole learning loop in my house. I can make a dataset, train Gemma 4, test an idea, find my mistake, and try again without renting a GPU or sending the work somewhere else.</p>
  <p>If a small model can learn a made-up association like this, perhaps it can learn a phone-sized assistant’s preferred vocabulary, a field technician’s shorthand, a child’s invented game, or a private tool’s tiny command language. Those are only starting points. What would you teach it?</p>
</section>
</div>
